Assassin's Creed unity is a familiar  game that makes some big changes sadly  not all of them are for the better that  doesn't mean there's not a lot to like  take the new lead on oh he's certainly  one of the most charismatic and well  active Assassin's Creed leads I've seen  in a while  [ __ ] Shaw mischievous and just enough of  a jerk to still be lovable but the story  never quite exploits his charms to the  fullest  it certainly tries though unity shiz  away from having a vast collection of  ancillary characters focusing instead  upon its lead Arno and his love interest  in Lee's but less of those supporting  characters around many of whom tended to  play a more lighthearted role the story  takes on a much darker tone just go  please  what other direction could have taken  the complex Abstergo storyline as long  sinks jump the shark and unity only  briefly ties itself into the wider  Assassin's Creed universe if you are  hoping for a deeper dive into the  modelled mysteries of Abstergo you won't  find it here the story which evolved  some love story to revenge tale to  murder mystery and then circles right  back to love story again is swiftly  paced but that all-important chemistry  between its two leads isn't strong  enough to tie the whole thing together I  cared enough to want to see things  through to the end but not enough to be  truly saddened by any of its events it's  a shame that the story doesn't tie into  the setting that deeply particularly  since unities betrayal of a paris  scarred by the civil unrest of the  French Revolution is one of its greatest  triumphs the attention to detail that's  gone into the city is nothing short of  astonishing exploring Paris's many  districts you realize that the sheer  scale of the city is incredible not only  in terms of its explorable limits but in  the huge number of citizens wandering  its streets a mission where you're  tasked with performing an assassination  among a crowd of thousands shows how  unity is as close as the series has ever  come to creating a tangible convincing  city  unity succeeds in his combat and  missions two gone is the enforced  hand-holding of old replace with  excellent sandbox style missions that  let you take on an assassination however  you like you might not succeed every  time but I'm a fan for trial and error  approach planning out the perfect  assassination route after many attempts  the replayability factor here is huge -  giving you far more of an incentive to  go back and nail those bonus conditions  for completing a mission these sand  boxes make up the bulk of unities  missions as well as the all-new 4 player  co-op mode and you know what  co-op is ok if not the go to multiplayer  mode you might have hoped for those fun  are the heist missions where your team  is rewarded the stealthiness the more  people you alert along the way the less  cash you get at the end the ability to  highlight potential kills for your  teammates as well as unlocked skills  like shared eagle vision do a great job  of reinforcing an idea of teamwork  indeed the most successful and  entertaining heist come from putting  together a very team with some members  focusing on close-range attacks and  others taking point with long-range  weapons integrating the coop into the  single-player campaign is a nice idea  and it makes some more cohesive ties  into the storyline but it also means  that the carp is a mere sidelines of the  action rather than something you'd want  to plow a lot of time into sadly the  excellent PvP multiplayer from previous  games doesn't make a return  so what else is new there's a mechanic  that creates a visible silhouette of  your last-known position making it  easier to know exactly where you've been  spotted and it also acts as a law for  enemies  there are also time anomaly missions to  play through which are neat platforming  puzzles set in different Harry the time  there's no more crafting to worry about  with skill points and hard cash lesson  you customize Arno to your heart's  content  while more freeform parkour movement  makes of fluid climbing even if it's  sometimes frustratingly flakes out and  puts you right in the path of an enemy  there are other irritations to this  content that has to be unlocked by the  web-based assassin's creed initiatory  painfully dull Assassin's Creed  companion app there are glitches that  cause MPC's to randomly walk in midair  or Arno to float between jumps and  despite whatever colloquial reasons  Ubisoft gives it's really weird to  wander around revolutionary Paris and  have every conversation play out in a  thick British accent know your heart is  set on keeping the peace but bringing  Monsieur de Lesseps killer to justice  would count for something wouldn't it  yes it would but do not confuse your  personal vendetta with a sound strategy  if he wants to kill Templars we don't  kill Templars not all of unities more  progressive touches work out for the  best but you might spend more time  noticing what's old than what's new the  terrific atmosphere of Paris the focus  on parkour and the incentives performing  stealthy assassinations all these things  hint at a game that's trying to return  to its roots  after branching out so wildly in its  past two iterations  yes unity is the most assassin's creed  to light of the series since well as  Sasson screed - and while it never hits  the dizzying heights of Ezio jaunt  through 15th century Italy unity  similarities are comforting enough to  take the edge off it's less than  successful changes is unity the next-gen  Assassin's Creed we've all been waiting  for  well not quite it's very good maybe even  great in places but the story's smaller  focus has come at the expense of this  exquisitely rendered backdrop assassin's  creed should be all about grandness and  spectacle and sadly that's sorely  lacking here  you
 

